{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"EXECUTION DECISION — reuse the repo's VERIFIED-FAKE HONESTY CONTRACT rather than writing parallel shape assertions.\nFOUND: tests/_jira_shape_contract.py (epic f89d, story 59ef) is already the SINGLE definition of the return shapes the Cloud enrichment surface must produce — assert_search_shape, assert_comment_map_shape, assert_issuelinks_map_shape, assert_parent_map_shape. Its docstring states the design: the same assertions run against BOTH the hermetic FakeAcliClient (tests/integration/.../test_verified_fake_contract.py) and the REAL AcliClient against live Jira (tests/external/test_verified_fake_contract_live.py), 'so the fake cannot drift from the real client undetected'. It deliberately asserts STRUCTURE/TYPE ONLY, never values — explicitly anti-change-detector.\nDECISION: J6's DC transport is held to those SAME functions, not to a new parallel set. Three reasons:\n 1. This story's core claim is that the library's object model is unwrapped at the boundary — 'every method returns the RAW payload shapes rebar's existing mappers already consume, so nothing downstream learns about jira.Issue'. The existing contract is precisely the definition of those shapes, so reusing it tests the actual claim instead of a restatement of it.\n 2. A parallel set of DC shape assertions could drift from the Cloud ones, which would silently permit exactly the two-implementations-diverging failure this epic exists to prevent — and it would be invisible, because both suites would be green.\n 3. It extends the established verified-fake pattern to a third implementation (Cloud fake, Cloud real, DC real) at no new design cost.\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE TESTS: the DC unit tests drive a fake pycontribs client and assert the transport's returns through these shared functions; the live test (tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_transport.py) drives the REAL transport against the J5 harness and asserts through the SAME functions. If the DC transport ever leaks a jira.Issue instead of a raw dict, both tiers fail on a shared assertion rather than on a DC-specific one I invented.\nNOTE the shape contract is imported via the tests/ sys.path entry (tests/conftest.py), as its own docstring records."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785428710328069001,"uuid":"32f4f388-df3d-4d3e-a911-5db8376a2447"}